{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Peer review typically filters claims before publication, whereas DeepReason accepts positions into the pool and filters them post-hoc.","Peer reviewers are human and prone to fatigue, whereas DeepReason's critics are algorithmic with fixed capacity constraints."],"overturn_conditions":["If a triage policy in peer review is shown to effectively reduce reviewer burden without compromising quality, a similar lightweight filter could bypass the full batched criticism mechanism in DeepReason.","If studies show peer review quality is insensitive to submission volume, the dilution analogy fails."],"shared_structure":["A set of candidate claims (rival positions) are generated and must be critiqued by external reviewers (foreign schools).","Limited reviewer capacity (batch criticism budget) creates a bottleneck where per-claim attention decreases as the pool of claims grows."],"source_memory_refs":["en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peer_review"],"transfer_claims":["Just as an overload of submissions in peer review reduces the rigor of per-paper review and slows the rejection of flawed work, an overload of rival positions in DeepReason dilutes criticism weight and stalls the retirement of flawed positions."]},"claim":"The near-zero retirement rate as rivalry grows from 18 to ~60 is caused by a coverage-debt dilution effect: under fixed per-batch criticism budgets, the critical weight assigned to each rival position decays as the number of targets increases, preventing any single position from accumulating enough attacks to cross the retirement threshold.","counterconditions":["The critic's per-batch budget scales directly with the number of rival positions N.","The retirement threshold is dynamically adjusted downward as the total number of rival positions increases.","The system contains an architectural bug that resets accumulated coverage debt at the end of each batch rather than carrying it forward."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"09ffabcd92979168","quote":"# Pass 1: attack (Dung grounded extension; unique, skeptical, polynomial)"},{"block":"0974daebd1665965","quote":"**Closure rule:** any attacker of `validity_node` attacks the warrant (hence its carrier's attack edge)."},{"block":"0efee9d35ff76f87","quote":"# inequivalent survivors only"}],"mechanism":"Foreign-school criticism is applied in batches. Let B be the per-batch critique budget and N be the number of rival positions. The expected critique weight per position is B/N. As N grows from 18 to 60, the per-position critique frequency decreases. If the retirement threshold T is fixed, and if some of the critique weight is spent on already-attacked warrants without reaching the threshold (coverage debt), the effective time to retire any single position increases non-linearly. This dilution means the system reaches a dynamic equilibrium where new positions are generated faster than existing ones can accumulate sufficient criticism-weighted attention to be retired.","optional_refs":["src-74ded11eccee525366948958d3b4fb57bccbdeb2"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-74ded11eccee525366948958d3b4fb57bccbdeb2"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8}],"simulation_proposals":[]}